# Canonical author identity.
#
# Every commit in this repository is by one person. The name recorded in the
# commit objects changed over time — the project was called GroundState before
# it was called Bloch, and some commits were made by build and PMO automation
# running under the same author. This file maps all of those to the canonical
# identity, so `git log`, `git shortlog` and `git blame` show one contributor
# instead of six.
#
# It is deliberately a mailmap and not a history rewrite. Rewriting would change
# every commit hash from the genesis commit onward, break every clone, and
# invalidate the dozens of commit references cited in docs/, ADRs and
# post-mortems. The old names in the objects are the record of what actually
# happened; this file is how the record is read, not an edit to it.
#
# Note the one thing it does not do: GitHub's web interface renders the raw
# author from the commit object and does not consult .mailmap, so commits
# already pushed keep displaying their original name there. Git itself, and
# every tool that uses it, honours this file.

